-- title:
--   Second row of the Drinfeld linear part is (0,1)
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime, $M'$ a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and $\ell$ a prime with $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell \mid M'$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero that is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of order $q\ell$, let $\zeta \in L$ be a primitive $q$-th root of unity and $\xi \in L$ a primitive $q\ell$-th root of unity with $\zeta = \xi^{\ell}$. Let $H_1 \le (\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^{\times}$ be the intersection of the kernel of reduction to $(\mathbb{Z}/q)^{\times}$ with the kernel of reduction to $(\mathbb{Z}/\ell)^{\times}$, and let $K$ be the intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ obtained by adjoining to $L$ the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion function field of $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$, with $q \in \mathfrak{m}_A$, with $\zeta$ in the image of $A$, with uniformiser $\varpi$ generating $\mathfrak{m}_A$, and with $K$ an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$; let $j \in K$ be nonzero with Laurent expansion the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant. Write $\mathfrak{X} =$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236), the pushout gluing $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the integral closure of $A[j]$ in $K$ to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the integral closure of $A[j^{-1}]$ in $K$. Let $z$ be a point of $\mathfrak{X}$ such that the germ $\varpi_z$ of the image of $\varpi$ under the structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk $\mathcal{O}_{\mathfrak{X},z}$, and let $y$ be a point of the $j$-finite chart with $\iota_{\mathrm{Fin}}(y) = z$ whose residue $j$-invariant is supersingular in the sense of [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7): for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from the $j$-finite chart algebra to $\Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ is such that every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero point killed by $q$. Let $W$ be a complete discrete valuation domain, $\sigma : A \to W$ a ring homomorphism with $\mathfrak{m}_W = (\sigma\varpi)$, and $f, u, v \in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u, v$ units and $f \equiv X_0X_1^{q} - X_0^{q}X_1 \pmod{(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}}$. Let $e$ be a ring isomorphism from the $\mathfrak{m}$-adic completion of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathfrak{X},z}$ onto $S := W[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma\varpi)v - fu)$, and assume the branch-anchor clause `hanchor`: writing $\mathrm{germ}_Y$ for the germ map at $z$ from the $j$-finite chart algebra into the stalk and $\mathrm{mk}_S$ for the quotient map, for every prime $P \subset S$ not containing both $X_0$ and $X_1$, containing $C(\sigma\varpi)$, and containing $X_0 + h$ for some $h \in (X_0,X_1)^2$, an element $a$ of the chart algebra has $\mathrm{germ}_Y(a)$ mapping into $e^{-1}(P)$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the image of $\mathfrak{m}_A$ in $L$. Then, for all ring automorphisms $\sigma_L$ of $L$ and $\sigma_A$ of $A$ compatible with $A \to L$ and with $\sigma_A \equiv \mathrm{id} \pmod{\mathfrak{m}_A}$, every ring automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ acting coefficientwise through $\sigma_L$ on Laurent series and preserving the $j$-finite chart algebra, every ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$ satisfying $\theta(e(\mathrm{germ}_Y(a))) = e(\mathrm{germ}_Y(\tau a))$ for all $a$ in that chart algebra, and every matrix $M \in M_2(W)$ with $\theta(\mathrm{mk}_S X_k) \equiv \mathrm{mk}_S\big(\sum_i C(M_{ik})X_i\big)$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by $\mathrm{mk}_S X_0, \mathrm{mk}_S X_1$ for $k = 0,1$: one has $M_{10} \in \mathfrak{m}_W$ and $M_{11} - 1 \in \mathfrak{m}_W$.
--
--   This computes the second row of the linear part of an inertial automorphism of the Drinfeld local chart at a supersingular point of the two-chart integral model, in the auxiliary level setting with a guard prime $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ dividing $M'$: modulo $\mathfrak{m}_W$ that row is $(0,1)$, the anchoring hypothesis identifying the branch $X_0 = 0$ with the cusp branch on which $\tau$ acts trivially to first order. It feeds the companion statement that the linear part of such an automorphism is the diagonal matrix with lower entry $1$.
